# Simple RAG Project - Cursor Rules

## Project Overview

This is a full-stack Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) application using:

- Backend: NestJS 11 + LangChain + PostgreSQL with pgvector
- Frontend: React 19 + TanStack Router + Tailwind CSS + Shadcn UI
- Auth: Auth0 with JWT
- Database: PostgreSQL with pgvector extension
- Monorepo: pnpm workspaces

## Code Style and Quality

### General Principles

- Write clean, maintainable, and self-documenting code
- Follow SOLID principles and design patterns
- Prefer composition over inheritance
- Keep functions small and focused (single responsibility)
- Use descriptive variable and function names
- Add comments only when the code cannot be self-explanatory

### TypeScript

- Use strict TypeScript - avoid `any` types (this is critical technical debt to address)
- Prefer interfaces over types for object shapes
- Use type inference where possible
- Always define return types for functions
- Use const assertions where appropriate
- Enable strict mode in tsconfig.json (currently disabled, needs gradual migration)

### Naming Conventions

- Use PascalCase for classes, interfaces, types, and components
- Use camelCase for variables, functions, and methods
- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constants
- Use kebab-case for file names (except React components)
- Prefix interfaces with descriptive nouns (e.g., `User`, not `IUser`)
- Prefix boolean variables with is/has/should (e.g., `isActive`, `hasPermission`)
- Never use single-character variable names (except in very short loops where context is obvious, like `i`, `j` for array indices)
- Use descriptive names that reveal intent (e.g., `userId` not `id`, `recipeList` not `list`, `totalCount` not `count`)
- Avoid abbreviations unless they are well-known (e.g., `url`, `api`, `dto` are acceptable)
- Use meaningful names for function parameters (e.g., `getUserById(userId: string)` not `getUserById(id: string)`)

## Backend (NestJS)

### Architecture

- Follow NestJS module pattern - keep modules cohesive
- Use dependency injection for all services
- Implement DTOs with Zod validation for all endpoints
- Use TypeORM entities for database models
- Keep controllers thin - business logic belongs in services
- Use proper HTTP status codes and exceptions

### Services

- Services should contain business logic
- Use transactions for multi-step database operations
- Handle errors gracefully with custom exceptions
- Log important operations with Winston logger (avoid console.log)
- Keep services testable and mockable

### Database

- Use TypeORM query builder or repositories (avoid raw SQL where possible)
- Use transactions for operations that modify multiple tables
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
- Add proper indexes for performance
- Use migrations for schema changes (migrate to TypeORM migrations from custom MigrationService)

### Security

- Validate all inputs with Zod schemas
- Use JWT authentication with Auth0
- Implement RBAC with @Permissions decorator
- Sanitize user inputs to prevent XSS and injection attacks
- Use rate limiting for expensive operations (like RAG queries)
- Never log sensitive information (tokens, passwords, API keys)

### Error Handling

- Use NestJS built-in exceptions (BadRequestException, NotFoundException, etc.)
- Create custom exception classes for domain-specific errors
- Always catch and handle errors in services
- Return meaningful error messages to clients
- Log errors with appropriate severity levels

### Testing

- Write unit tests for all services and complex logic
- Write integration tests for controllers and database operations
- Use mocking for external dependencies
- Aim for meaningful test coverage (not just "should be defined")
- Test error paths and edge cases

## Frontend (React + TanStack)

### Components

- Use functional components with hooks
- Keep components small and focused
- Use proper TypeScript types (avoid `any`)
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Use Shadcn UI components for consistent UI
- Prefer composition over prop drilling

### State Management

- Use TanStack Query for server state
- Use Zustand for client-side global state
- Keep state as local as possible
- Use React Context sparingly

### Routing

- Use TanStack Router for type-safe routing
- Define routes in the routes directory
- Use route loaders for data fetching
- Implement proper loading and error states

### Data Fetching

- Use TanStack Query hooks (useQuery, useMutation)
- Handle loading, error, and success states
- Implement proper error boundaries
- Use optimistic updates where appropriate
- Cache data appropriately

### Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS utility classes
- Follow mobile-first responsive design
- Use CSS variables for theming
- Keep styles consistent with design system
- Use Shadcn UI component variants

### Forms

- Use TanStack Form for form management
- Validate with Zod schemas
- Show clear error messages
- Provide loading states for submissions
- Handle API errors gracefully

### Performance

- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Implement virtualization for long lists
- Lazy load routes and components
- Optimize images and assets
- Avoid unnecessary re-renders

## API Design

### REST Endpoints

- Use RESTful conventions (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE)
- Use plural nouns for resources (e.g., `/users`, `/recipes`)
- Use proper HTTP status codes
- Implement pagination for list endpoints
- Use query parameters for filtering and sorting

### Request/Response

- Validate all request bodies with Zod DTOs
- Return consistent response formats
- Include proper error messages
- Use camelCase for JSON keys
- Document endpoints with JSDoc comments (add Swagger/OpenAPI)

### Authentication

- Protect endpoints with @UseGuards(JwtAuthGuard)
- Implement RBAC with @Permissions decorator
- Validate JWT tokens properly
- Handle expired tokens gracefully
- Use @CurrentUser decorator for user context

## Git Workflow

### Commits

- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Use conventional commits format (feat, fix, chore, docs, etc.)
- Keep commits atomic and focused
- Reference issue numbers where applicable

### Branches

- Use feature branches for new development
- Use descriptive branch names (e.g., `feature/user-management`, `fix/auth-bug`)
- Keep branches short-lived
- Rebase on main before merging

### Code Review

- Request reviews for all changes
- Address review comments promptly
- Keep PRs small and focused
- Include tests with code changes
- Update documentation as needed

## Documentation

### Code Documentation

- Add JSDoc comments for public APIs
- Document complex algorithms and business logic
- Keep documentation up-to-date with code changes
- Document environment variables and configuration
- Add README files for new modules

### API Documentation

- Add Swagger/OpenAPI documentation (currently missing)
- Document request/response schemas
- Include example requests and responses
- Document authentication requirements
- Document rate limiting and quotas

## Environment and Configuration

### Environment Variables

- Never commit sensitive data (.env files)
- Use Zod schemas for environment validation (env.shema.ts - fix typo)
- Document required environment variables in README
- Use different configs for dev/staging/prod
- Use .env.example for reference

## Technical Debt Priority

When making changes, prioritize addressing these technical debt items:

1. **High Priority**

   - Replace `any` types with proper TypeScript types
   - Enable strict mode in TypeScript configuration
   - Replace console.log with proper Winston logging
   - Add comprehensive test coverage
   - Standardize error handling patterns

2. **Medium Priority**

   - Migrate to TypeORM migrations from custom MigrationService
   - Standardize database query patterns (prefer TypeORM over raw SQL)
   - Add caching layer (Redis) for permissions and frequently accessed data
   - Add Swagger/OpenAPI documentation
   - Use transactions consistently for multi-step operations

3. **Low Priority**
   - Fix filename typo: env.shema.ts � env.schema.ts
   - Remove eslint-disable comments after fixing type issues
   - Add architecture decision records (ADRs)
   - Improve inline documentation

## Security Guidelines

- Never expose sensitive data in API responses
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
- Implement proper CORS configuration
- Use rate limiting for expensive operations
- Keep dependencies up-to-date
- Use environment variables for secrets
- Implement proper session management
- Log security events for monitoring

## Performance Guidelines

- Use database indexes for frequently queried fields
- Implement pagination for large datasets
- Use lazy loading and code splitting
- Optimize images and assets
- Use caching for frequently accessed data
- Monitor and optimize slow queries
- Use connection pooling for databases
- Implement proper error handling to prevent cascading failures

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML elements
- Add proper ARIA labels
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Use sufficient color contrast
- Add alt text for images
- Support screen readers
- Test with accessibility tools

## Monitoring and Logging

- Use Winston for structured logging
- Log important events and errors
- Include request IDs for tracing
- Monitor application health with /health endpoints
- Track API performance metrics
- Set up alerts for critical errors
- Use appropriate log levels (error, warn, info, debug)

## Dependencies

- Keep dependencies up-to-date
- Review security advisories regularly
- Avoid unnecessary dependencies
- Use exact versions in package.json for production
- Document why each major dependency is needed

## When Adding New Features

1. Plan the architecture and data models
2. Write tests first (TDD where appropriate)
3. Implement with proper error handling
4. Add proper TypeScript types (no `any`)
5. Add logging for important operations
6. Update documentation
7. Add API documentation (Swagger)
8. Test edge cases and error scenarios
9. Perform security review
10. Update relevant README sections

## AI Assistant Guidelines

When using AI assistants (like Cursor AI):

- Request proper TypeScript types, not `any`
- Ask for error handling and validation
- Request tests along with implementation
- Verify security implications
- Request documentation updates
- Ask about performance considerations
- Ensure accessibility is considered
- Follow existing patterns in the codebase
